  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1143 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe above-average temperatures and mostly dry weather looks to roll \non for the next few days for the vast majority of the forecast area. \nHigh temperatures should not approach record territory this week, \nthough we expect high temperatures generally 5-10 degrees above \naverage for the plains. Model guidance including ensemble clustering \ndata remains in relatively good agreement through Friday on the \nevolution of the overall upper-level pattern. \n\nA weak front has pushed through the area this evening, which will \nlead to high temperatures a touch cooler for Monday. There will be \nincreased cloud cover overnight through Monday morning. The \nmountains could see some isolated rain/snow showers on Monday \nafternoon as moisture increases. The central mountains look to have \nthe highest chances overall, though PoPs remain low. Little to no \naccumulation is expected. It will be rinse and repeat on Tuesday and \nWednesday with some isolated rain/snow showers (at best) possible \nfor the southern half of the high country. Temperatures should bump \nup a touch on both days as an upper-level ridge builds.\n\nAn upper-level low over the Pacific Ocean is expected to start to \napproach California on Wednesday, move inland, and be centered over \nUtah by Friday morning. The latest data shows a broadening trough \nmoving into WY and MT on Friday afternoon. As this low approaches, \nflow aloft is expected to turn to the southwest for our area and \nstay southwest as the trough moves off to our northwest. Late \nWednesday into Thursday, we expect deeper moisture to approach our \nsouthern and western high country. Precipitation chances should \nincrease from south to north across the high country starting \nWednesday evening and lasting through at least Friday morning with \nsome low-impact snow possible (i.e. below advisory level) mainly \nabove 11,500 ft or higher. By Thursday afternoon, we could start to \nsee some low rain chances creep into the plains before drier \nconditions return for Friday. Southwest flow aloft is generally an \nunfavorable setup for the metro to receive any significant rainfall, \nso any rain would likely be light and scattered in nature. This is \nreflected in the latest ensemble data with mean QPF amounts under \n2/10 inch for KDEN and higher QPF confined to the high country. \nDeterministic runs are even drier than that. \n\nFor the weekend, model disagreement continues. There are some signs \nin the ensemble data that temperatures could dip to near-average or \neven a tick below average for Saturday onward. This would be \ncourtesy of a cold front moving in from the north. Some low PoPs \ncould continue on Saturday and Sunday for the high country, though \nconfidence is low this far out.",
